WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.490
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:06.220
And today we're learning more
about McDonald v. Chicago,

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.730
a 2010 Supreme Court case

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.400
challenging a handgun ban
in the City of Chicago.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.790
The question at issue

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.060
was whether the 14th
Amendment's Due Process

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.600
or Privileges or Immunities Clause

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.590
means that the Second Amendment right

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:25.030
to keep and bear arms for
the purposes of self-defense

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.000
is applicable to the states.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.490
Or, put more plainly,

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.830
the Second Amendment says
the federal government

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.510
can't infringe on citizens
rights to keep and bear arms,

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.200
but can state governments?

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.640
To learn more about McDonald v. Chicago,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.160
I talked to two experts.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.900
Alan Gura is a lawyer who
argued McDonald v. Chicago

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.480
before the Supreme Court.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.270
Elizabeth Wydra is a
Supreme Court litigator

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.120
and the president

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.760
of the Constitutional
Accountability Center.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.810
So, Miss Wydra, could
you set the stage for us?

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.600
Who were the challengers in this case

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.280
and why did they decide to sue?

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.690
- [Elizabeth] The lead
plaintiff was Otis McDonald,

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:05.350
then a 76-year-old African-American

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:06.730
retired maintenance engineer

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.570
who lived in a part of Chicago

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.430
and wanted to have a gun for self-defense,

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.190
a handgun,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.380
to protect himself and
his family at his home.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.320
He lived in a neighborhood

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.110
that had grown increasingly troubled

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.350
with gang and drug-related violence

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.430
and he felt that he
wanted to have a handgun

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.210
to protect his wife and his family

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.420
after his home had been
broken into several times.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.380
But because the City of Chicago

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.730
had strict rules barring handguns,

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.710
he and three other Chicago residents

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.070
brought their case into court,

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.760
asking the Court to find

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.590
that the individual right
to keep and bear arms,

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.120
including a handgun for
self-defense in the home

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.060
that had been recognized
just a few years earlier

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.710
by the Supreme Court with respect
to the federal government,

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.550
also applies to the states
and cities like Chicago.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.530
- [Alan] In 2008,

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.860
the Supreme Court had held

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.620
that the Second Amendment
to the Constitution

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.700
guarantees Americans
the right to have guns

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.600
for the purpose of self-defense.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.200
But that case took
place in Washington, DC,

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.360
and Washington, DC,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.710
is sort of a unique creature in our law.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:15.940
It's a federal city,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.080
it's not located in any particular state,

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.010
and so everything that the
City of Washington does

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.900
is essentially done by
the federal government

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.530
or a unit of the federal government

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.550
to which the Bill of
Rights applies directly.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:34.900
But, historically,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.980
the Bill of Rights was never
thought to apply directly

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.360
to limit what cities and states could do.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.160
- [Kim] So, what were some of
the competing interpretations

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.350
that were at issue in this case?

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:50.360
- [Alan] Well, there were
a number of interpretations

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.420
that were at issue.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.430
The 14th Amendment has
several parts to it.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.720
In the first section of the 14th Amendment

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.700
it states that no state
shall make or enforce any law

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.680
which shall abridge the
privileges or immunities

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.920
of citizens of the United States.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.700
And then it also says, among other things,

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.020
that the states can't
deprive people of due process

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.100
when it comes time to regulating

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.730
their life, liberty, or property.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.690
States may not deprive anyone
of life, liberty, or property

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.530
without due process of law.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.880
When it comes to your basic civil rights,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.080
the question had always been

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.720
whether or not the states are bound

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.670
not to violate your civil rights

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.810
because they're told not to abridge

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.160
the privileges or immunities
of American citizens,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.660
or are your rights not to be violated

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.010
because the states can't deprive
you of due process of law.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.420
- [Elizabeth] So, when the
drafters of the 14th Amendment

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.260
were coming together

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.480
to think about what
rights would be protected

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:54.900
in the 14th Amendment,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.150
they looked at a a lot of the
abuses by state governments

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:01.850
of key rights that had
been previously protected

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.900
against federal government
action in the Bill of Rights.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.120
And when they were debating

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.110
what rights would be
included in these protections

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.990
against state action,

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.790
they talked repeatedly
about the importance

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:17.060
of the right to keep and bear arms.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.410
But the way they talked about it

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.930
was very different from the way

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:24.680
that we think about the 18th
century Revolutionary Founders

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.860
talking about the right
to keep and bear arms.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.270
In that case it was very
much about the militia

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.480
and preventing the tyranny
of government action.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.230
In the 14th Amendment context,

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.340
the right to keep and bear arms

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.450
was very much an individual right.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.520
They were concerned about freed persons

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.810
being subject to white militia violence

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.660
and being able to protect themselves

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.050
with a gun in their house.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:49.830
And so that was really one of the ways

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.710
in which they wanted to
protect the individual right

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:53.670
to keep and bear arms,

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:54.710
different from the way

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:56.730
that I think the Founders thought about it

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.250
when they were drafting
the Second Amendment.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.000
- [Kim] Yeah, this is a
really interesting point

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.960
because you can kinda see how
these things evolve over time.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.820
And particularly in the
1860s after the Civil War,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:12.440
there are many local or state statutes,

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:13.640
know as the Black Codes,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.610
that specifically prevented
newly-freed African-Americans

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.180
from owning firearms
or any kind of weapon.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.013
- [Elizabeth] Exactly,

00:05:21.013 --> 00:05:23.890
and that was a concern because,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:26.220
one, it was a right that
they considered to be

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:27.590
a right of citizenship.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:29.810
But two, and more practically
and more immediately

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:32.010
of concern for the people
who were being targeted

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.870
by this racial violence,

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:37.500
it was something that was
a question of life or death

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.430
for them.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.980
And the debates on this

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.040
by the drafters of the 14th Amendment

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.030
were very clear and very colorful.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:48.470
Senator Samuel Pomeroy
described how indispensable

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:50.580
the right to keep and bear arms was

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.560
and how important it was
that the 14th Amendment

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:53.580
protect that right.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.507
He said during the debates,

00:05:55.507 --> 00:05:58.007
"Every man should have
the right to bear arms

00:05:58.007 --> 00:06:01.307
"for the defense of himself
and family and his homestead.

00:06:01.307 --> 00:06:04.637
"And if the cabin door of
the freedman is broken open

00:06:04.637 --> 00:06:05.987
"and the intruder enters

00:06:05.987 --> 00:06:09.547
"for purposes as vile as
were known to slavery,

00:06:09.547 --> 00:06:11.117
"then should a well-loaded musket

00:06:11.117 --> 00:06:12.617
"be in the hand of the occupant

00:06:12.617 --> 00:06:15.037
"to send the polluted
wretch to another world

00:06:15.037 --> 00:06:18.130
"where his wretchedness will
forever remain complete."

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.460
- [Kim] So, going back
to the McDonald case,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.650
how did the Court rule in this case?

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.640
- [Alan] In McDonald,
we made two arguments

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:26.980
for why we should win.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:28.210
The first argument we made

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.090
was the historically more obvious one,

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:35.280
although it's one that courts
had not often entertained.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.420
In essence, the argument says

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.780
that when the 14th Amendment provided

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:41.640
that people cannot be deprived

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.600
of the privileges or immunities
of American citizenship,

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.180
that means they couldn't be deprived

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:49.020
of their basic fundamental rights.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:49.853
Right there in text,

00:06:49.853 --> 00:06:51.460
the 14th Amendment tells you

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:53.160
that you can't be deprived

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.850
of the rights of American citizenship.

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:59.590
Secondly, we made the argument that,

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:00.610
and this is the argument

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.320
that the courts have been
more open to over the years,

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:04.210
we made the argument

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.580
that where the state is prohibited

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:10.470
from depriving Otis McDonald
and other Chicago residents

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.600
of due process of law,

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.700
you can't deprive somebody of due process,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.270
that means not just that the state

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:17.850
can't do things arbitrarily,

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.580
not just that Otis McDonald

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.740
is entitled to some procedural protections

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.340
in the way that he's regulated

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:26.140
but also that there are some results,

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.600
some things that simply cannot be done.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.550
In taking away someone's firearms

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:34.413
in violation of the
Second Amendment rights,

00:07:34.413 --> 00:07:38.170
it's not something that the state can do,

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:41.740
there's no procedure that
the state could create

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:42.870
to achieve that.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:44.620
They would still be considered

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.190
to be giving somebody due process of law.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.680
- [Elizabeth] So, the Court
said what we have done

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.980
when we've looked at individual rights

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.060
under the 14th Amendment,

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.280
and whether or not original
Bill of Rights rights

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:57.870
that were initially understood

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.540
to limit the federal government
applied to the states,

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:03.140
it's looked at whether
or not they are part

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.700
of what we generally talk about

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:09.480
as fundamental to our
scheme of ordered liberty.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.160
Or, stated another day,

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:14.520
the question is whether
the right is firmly rooted

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.970
in this nation's history and tradition.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:18.410
And when they looked at that question

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.220
with respect to the right
to keep and bear arms,

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.500
they looked at a lot of
the reconstruction history

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.520
and said this was clearly something

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.600
that the drafters of the 14th Amendment

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.650
considered to be very
fundamental to ordered liberty

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:35.490
and thought that indeed the freed men,

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:37.750
the African-American people of the South

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.460
who had been subject to the slave power

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:42.820
and people across the country

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.850
who had been subject to
discrimination from the states

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.860
should be able to look to the Constitution

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.610
to protect the right to keep and bear arms

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:50.930
as their individual right.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.560
Just as the Court had
held in the Heller case,

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:56.290
they could be able to
look to the Constitution

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:59.060
for protection of that right
against the federal government.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:00.460
- [Kim] So, in the McDonald case,

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:02.840
how did the majority of the Court rule?

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:06.260
- [Alan] The justices broke
down in multiple different ways.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:09.640
There were four justices,
led by Justice Alito,

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:12.190
who refused to look any further

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.470
at the Privileges or Immunities Clause.

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:19.030
They thought it was old, it
was water under the bridge.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:21.450
They were not interested in looking to see

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:25.740
whether or not the Court's
erasing of that provision

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:26.660
was legitimate,

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.620
even if it's widely agreed
to be illegitimate today.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:31.130
But the Court didn't wanna go there.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:33.850
And instead those four
justices, led by Justice Alito,

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:38.600
held that the due process clause

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:40.930
incorporates the Second Amendment right

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:41.870
to keep and bear arms

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:42.810
as against the states

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:44.980
because it is a fundamental right,

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.740
it's deeply rooted in our
nation's history and traditions,

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:50.540
it's something that's very
basic to our understanding

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:53.190
of our civil and political institutions.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.090
Four justices thought

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.890
that the right to keep and
bear arms is fundamental.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:58.150
- [Elizabeth] So, there
was actually a debate

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:00.330
among the members of the majority

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.440
who all believed that there was a right

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:05.380
to have a handgun in the
house for self-defense

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.213
that was protected

00:10:06.213 --> 00:10:10.260
against state and local
government regulations.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:14.030
But Justice Thomas would
have found that right

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:15.610
through another means.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:20.330
He would have looked at the
Privileges or Immunities Clause

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:22.490
of the 14th Amendment

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:25.540
instead of the now accepted method

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:29.840
of looking at the due
process liberty clause

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:31.010
of the 14th Amendment,

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.560
which is how these other
provisions of the Bill of Rights

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.240
have been incorporated against the states.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.940
There were many arguments,

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:40.140
including I should say
a brief that I filed

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:44.570
on behalf of scholars from
across the ideological spectrum

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.040
who said that in fact

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:48.900
the Privileges or Immunities
Clause of the 14th Amendment

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:53.500
is the part of that
constitutional amendment

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.810
that was intended to protect

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:57.830
this particular substantive right

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.680
as well as the other substantive rights

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:02.330
of the Bill of Rights

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.180
against state infringement.

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:09.170
Justice Thomas' concurrence
did not get any other votes,

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:10.990
whether from the people who were against

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:12.030
the right to keep and bear arms

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:12.950
and those who didn't,

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:15.750
but it is an important contribution

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.900
to thinking about how we understand rights

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:23.070
and where they are protected
in the Constitution.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.830
- [Alan] Justice Thomas'
opinion in McDonald

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.940
is groundbreaking.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.260
It represents the first time since 1868

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:32.710
that the original meaning

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.170
of the 14th Amendment's
Privileges or Immunities Clause,

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:38.750
the basic protection that
we have for civil rights

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:39.800
in this country

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:41.860
against state and local governments

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:45.200
was decisive in a case
at the Supreme Court.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.200
It's been that long since a
true original understanding

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:52.730
of the 14th Amendment's language

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:56.290
has finally proven to be decisive.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:58.240
- [Elizabeth] There
were of course justices

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.000
who thought that there is
not an individual right

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.690
to have a handgun in the
home for self-defense.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:08.790
These were the same
justices, not surprisingly,

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.550
who thought that there wasn't
a Second Amendment right

00:12:12.529 --> 00:12:14.890
to be protected against
federal government action

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:16.880
to keep a handgun in the
home for self-defense.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.290
They would have thought that
this was much more a right

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:22.070
in the Second Amendment context

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:24.570
that had to do with militia
service, military service,

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:27.300
and therefore did not extend
to an individual right

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.180
to have a gun in the
home for self-defense.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:31.250
- [Alan] McDonald's really
a 14th Amendment case.

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:34.590
Guns happened to be the
things that was in it

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:37.410
but I'd always seen it primarily

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.200
as a 14th Amendment case.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.930
It's not really a case
that tells you anything

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:42.933
about the scope of the Second Amendment.

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:45.190
In fact, it doesn't say anything

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:46.120
about the scope of the Second Amendment,

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.460
very little.

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.550
- [Elizabeth] There are many
issues left to be resolved

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:51.420
in the Second Amendment context.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:53.030
The Heller case itself,

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:54.950
which first articulated

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:57.860
that there was a right for individuals

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:01.120
to keep and bear arms in
their homes for self-defense

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.930
made clear that it wasn't saying

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.740
that there could be no gun regulation.

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:08.150
That case, even though it was
written by Justice Scalia,

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:09.780
and certainly in the McDonald case

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.950
Justice Alito's majority
opinion reiterated this,

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:15.640
said that while there were certain laws

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:18.460
that would be struck
down as unconstitutional

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:20.040
because they prohibited the possession

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.640
of handguns in the home,

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:23.250
they recognized at the same time

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:24.660
that the right to keep and bear arms

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:28.430
is not a right to keep and
carry any weapon whatsoever

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.120
in any manner whatsoever

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:31.800
and for whatever purpose.

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:33.820
So given that the Court itself,

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:37.220
and even very conservative
justices recognized

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:41.040
that there could be and perhaps should be

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:42.660
common sense limitations

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:46.950
on both the types of
guns that are allowed,

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:50.290
the manner in which they are allowed,

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:53.280
and where they can be
taken and in what manner,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:57.580
then a lot of the gun
regulations that are being passed

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:02.130
are still very much part of
the constitutional debate.

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:05.270
We've seen frankly most
of the laws thus far

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:07.930
be upheld as constitutional laws

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:11.110
that for example target assault weapons,

00:14:11.110 --> 00:14:13.600
high-capacity magazines,

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.830
and certain licensing and
registration requirements.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:21.690
So I think we will see more
cases to define the contours

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:25.290
of the individual right keep and bear arms

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.860
as these gun regulations get passed

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:32.290
and as the courts get to pass
upon their constitutionality.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:35.220
- [Kim] So, we've learned
that the Supreme Court ruled

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.410
that the 14th Amendment's
Due Process Clause

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:40.880
that no state can deprive a citizen

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:44.860
of life, liberty, or property
without due process of law

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:48.160
did incorporate the Second
Amendment into the states

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:49.000
under the reasoning

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.230
that the right to keep and
bear arms for self-defense

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:56.130
is fundamental to the nation's
scheme of ordered liberty.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:57.750
According to Alan Gura,

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:00.200
even though this case was about guns,

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.450
it was really a 14th Amendment case

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:06.190
concerning whether states can
restrict citizens liberty.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:07.900
Elizabeth Wydra, however,

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:10.030
cautions that the decision in McDonald

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:13.370
didn't preclude any
restriction on gun ownership,

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:16.560
and she suspects that new
cases will continue to define

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:19.320
the individual right
to keep and bear arms.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.080
To learn more about this case,

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:23.260
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:24.880
Interactive Constitution,

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:28.513
and Khan Academy's resources
on US government and politics.

